{"author":{"id":"6c7374a500bfd4cb191ae45b3f9d4b7a3d9d7f0324d04add217e8cbefeb7e1b9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1792415530,"nonce":"NEEIopR5rOH1NrO4EYnHxI1SYPc=","message":"**Desktop memory while animating** (found from a report: about 1.05 GB with the music playing, about 600 MB once it stopped)\n\nThe footprint is the live heap (about 100-130 MB) plus the young garbage Ash leaves until its own threshold: each of its minor collections freed about 12,000 blocks, around 400 MB, every 7 seconds on Navigate and Vehicle, with a pause of 15-20 ms. While anything animates the loop never idles, so the idle collection that returns it does not run; with the music stopped on a still page it does, which is the drop. ASH_GC_TRIGGER_MB changed nothing in a run on Navigate.\n\nMemory.afterFrame (called after each drawn frame, desktop only) asks for a major collection, 6-16 ms, when the heap holds 96 MB more than after the last one it asked for, checked once a second. Navigate settles at about 620-650 MB instead of 1.1 GB, Vehicle at about 700-750 MB instead of 890 MB, and the frame rate is the same with and without it (Vehicle 36 fps both, worst gap 61 ms against 87 ms).\n\nStill large on those pages: the startup peak of the heap is not given back while frames keep coming, and the allocation itself is 60-80 MB/s on Navigate (the route canvas is tessellated again each frame for the moving light) and about 30 MB/s on Vehicle. Cutting that is the real fix; the governor bounds it.","files":null}]}